The [#4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471) says all of its reports are one issue, closed by change #29.
Its own table says otherwise. Run the desk's fingerprint over the merged reports, and they fall
into separate issues. Only one of them is the issue #4471 was opened for.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is closed with two issues inside it that nobody owns. Each one now has a ticket of its own.
</Callout>

## The issues and their tickets

<SplitTickets ticket={4471} {tickets} />

- **`sched-prod` `E-TZ-114`**: shifts show one hour late. This is #4471 itself. Change #29 is
  the fix the desk recorded; the ticket stays open until someone checks that it fixed the cause.
- **`tram-3` `E-TZ-114`**: departure boards run one hour behind. Same code, other place, other
  bug. Change #29 was a `sched-prod` fix; nothing says the boards were fixed.
- **`rota-6` `E-SYNC-04`**: a night shift listed twice, an hour apart, one copy with nobody on
  it. Not a shifted clock at all: a rota-sync duplicate in care homes.

## Why they were merged

The [triage rule](/kb/triage/dedupe) merges on the subject line
([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)). Every subject line that night came in
from more than one product: "Off by 1h" and "URGENT times wrong" read the same on a tram board
and on a care-home rota. Intake also guessed the product from the subject, so some tram and
rota reports were tagged `sched-prod` (the "tagged elsewhere" column). The place comes from the
reporter's tenant, not from that tag.

The fingerprint in `#lib/kb/fingerprint` already keeps them apart: place plus server code.
Decision 0001 is accepted, so it is not edited here. It needs a new record that replaces it.

## The first row is not a report

The earliest entry merged into #4471, `R-41200`, has no reporter, no subject and no source. Its
body is `status: clean (E-TZ-114)`. It is the job that ran at 03:12, not a customer, and it
raised the server code before the first customer report came in. The desk's table counts it as a
person; the table above does not.

